val-work.excerpt:
\paragraph{Work Shape ($\text{Work}_{\text{shape}}$)}
Work shape measures how much fitting changes each residue's absolute deviation from the mean log protection factor. We write this deviation as $a_i=|\log P_{f,i}-\overline{\log P_f}|$ and define the centred protection score $H_{\text{opt}}(X;i)=RTa_i$. Deviations above and below the mean contribute identically, so Work shape measures changes in protection contrast and not in its direction.

\begin{equation}
    \text{Work}_{\text{shape}}
    = \frac{RT}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}
    \left|
    \left|\log P_{f,i}-\overline{\log P_f}\right|
    -
    \left|\log \hat{P}_{f,i}-\overline{\log \hat{P}_f}\right|
    \right|
    \label{eq:work_shape}
\end{equation}


\paragraph{Work Scale ($\text{Work}_{\text{scale}}$)}
Work scale measures the shift in mean log protection factor during fitting. Changes in frame weights and in BV coefficients can both contribute to this shift.

\begin{equation}
    \text{Work}_{\text{scale}} = RT \left| \overline{\log P_f} - \overline{\log \hat{P}_f} \right|
    \label{eq:work_scale}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Work Density ($\text{Work}_{\text{density}}$)}
Work density measures the mean absolute change in residue-wise entropy-like contributions across the centred protection profile. Each residue is assigned a weight $w_{\text{opt}}(X;i)=e^{-a_i}\sum_j e^{-a_j}$, with the sum taken over the compared residues, and a contribution $S_{\text{opt}}(X;i)=-R\,w_{\text{opt}}(X;i)\log w_{\text{opt}}(X;i)$. Fitted quantities are calculated in the same way from the fitted profile. These weights are indexed over residues. They are not conformational populations, and they are distinct from the frame weights adjusted during reweighting. The weights are not normalised to sum to one, so individual contributions can be negative.

\begin{equation}
    \text{Work}_{\text{density}} = \frac{T}{1000N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left|S_{\text{opt}}(\hat{X};i)-S_{\text{opt}}(X;i)\right|
    \label{eq:work_density}
\end{equation}

val-limits.excerpt:
\subsection{Limitations}

The Best-Vendruscolo forward model relates protection factors to empirical counts of heavy-atom contacts and hydrogen bonds.\cite{best_structural_2006} Contact-based protection-factor models compress the structural determinants of exchange into these local counts. In heterogeneous ensembles and allosteric systems, exchange also depends on the populations and cooperative opening of distinct conformational states. In intrinsically disordered and non-native systems, transient contacts and solvent exposure challenge the transferability of this empirical mapping. These systems therefore require assessment of both conformational sampling and forward-model suitability. Work shape, scale, and density quantify the adjustments required under the chosen model; large adjustments motivate testing alternative ensembles and model parameterisations alongside validation error. An inadequate prior or forward model can drive fitted populations towards an incorrect structural interpretation. Reweighting cannot create conformational states that are absent from the prior ensemble. Conclusions therefore remain comparative across the candidate ensembles supplied.

The fitting forward model assumes EX2 exchange. Our synthetic kinetic benchmark tests departures from this assumption and shows that staged calibration can restore uptake agreement under severe kinetic misspecification (\autoref{fig:kinetic_calibration_overview}). Good uptake agreement did not imply recovery of the target populations with an ensemble containing both target conformational states. Calibration therefore does not validate the assumed exchange kinetics, and caution remains necessary when interpreting systems that may access EX1 kinetics.

val-iso.excerpt:
\subsubsection{Iso-Validation Experiments}

The TeaA trajectory used for the Iso-Validation experiment originates from the bias-exchange metadynamics simulations reported by Marinelli et al.\cite{marinelli_allosteric_2011} We used the unbiased replica representing equilibrium dynamics, accessed through the HDXer tutorial/data materials described by Bradshaw et al., which we cite for trajectory processing and HDXer methods.\cite{bradshaw_interpretation_2020} To produce the plots here we repeated the trajectory clustering on a sliced (1:50) subset of the original trajectory provided. Synthetic HDX-uptake curves were generated without added measurement noise to isolate the effects of ensemble composition from experimental uncertainty.

\subparagraph{Trajectory Clustering}

Trajectory clustering was performed by aligning all frames to each reference (Open and Closed) and computing the RMSD. Frames were sorted into Open and Closed clusters if structures were less than 1.0 \text{\AA} from one of the reference structures.


\paragraph{ISO-Bimodal Ensemble}

The ISO-Bimodal ensemble is formed by taking the structures directly from the clustering process. Its native open to closed ratio is 5:95. We reweighted it to 40:60 open to closed and predicted uptake from the reweighted populations, giving the synthetic target used for fitting.

\paragraph{ISO-Trimodal Ensemble}

The ISO-Trimodal ensemble is the parent ensemble of the clustered, ISO-Bimodal ensemble. This contains Open, Closed as well as intermediate structures.

